# The layers whose remainder is claimed to be on or over the line. One row per layer filing
# `interference` or `transition`: demand meets or exceeds supply somewhere in the range.
#
# The side where somebody bears something
#   Under a clearance the supply is above demand everywhere and there is nothing to absorb; here
#   there is, so the holders' shares have a size to add up to and the absorbing buffer has a size
#   to fit inside. checks/clearance_with_unserved.sqlc reads the layers this relation leaves out.
#
# Not the same question as what the buffer absorbed
#   On or over the line is not the same question as "what did the buffer absorb", and the two
#   relations here that weigh shares against a buffer, entries/borne.sqlc and
#   arithmetic/shares_bounded.sqlc, narrow it again to `interference` for exactly that reason;
#   entries/borne.sqlc says why. Under a `transition` both sides are live at once, and no single
#   size here says which one a share is on.
-- pm:Remainder/sign in {interference, transition}.
SELECT r.*
FROM (
    :compose(layers/remainder.sqlc)
) r
WHERE r.sign IN ('interference', 'transition')
